<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zamora</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zamora is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, capital of the province of Zamora. Fortified by the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> in 893, it was a key stronghold on the Christian frontier along the Douro and was besieged repeatedly in the following centuries. In 1143 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zamora">Treaty of Zamora</a> saw Alfonso VII of León recognise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> as king of Portugal. Its Romanesque churches and cathedral are among the finest in Spain.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fortified towns at <b>Zamora</b>, from 893, Simancas, Toro and Burgos anchored a frontier along the Douro, and the eastern march became known as Castile, the land of castles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sancho II defeated Alfonso at Llantada in 1068 and at Golpejera in 1072 and took León, but was murdered in October 1072 while besieging his sister Urraca's city of <b>Zamora</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Alfonso III (r. 866–910) the Asturian kings pushed south to the Douro, resettling the thinly populated plain with colonists from the north and from al-Andalus and fortifying towns such as <b>Zamora</b>, Toro and the old Roman camp of León.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Astures occupied the lands between the Cantabrian coast and the Douro, in modern Asturias, León and northern <b>Zamora</b>, from about the 4th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/astures">Astures</a></li><li><blockquote>The Vaccaei occupied the middle Douro valley, in modern Valladolid, Palencia, <b>Zamora</b> and Burgos, from about the 4th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/vaccaei">Vaccaei</a></li></ul></section>
